{
  "title": "Detecting silent agent failures",
  "content": "detecting agent has a habit of breaking in ways the monitoring wasn't watching for. This works through the problem properly, from cause to fix. Everyone assumes someone else already checked this.\n\nAn agent that returns a 200 OK with a plausible-looking answer is the hardest failure mode to catch. There is no exception, no stack trace, no red dashboard — just a confident paragraph that happens to be wrong, or subtly harmful, or a hallucinated citation that a customer will act on. This post is a build guide: a small, runnable quality gate that sits between your agent's output and whatever consumes it, so \"the agent responded\" stops being the same thing as \"the agent worked.\"\n\nThe mechanism is not novel. It is the same pattern used in content moderation and CI pipelines: define cheap, deterministic checks that catch the obvious failures first, then layer a model-based judge on top for the cases that need judgment. What makes it worth writing down is the ordering and the failure modes — most teams build the expensive judge first, wire it to a threshold they picked arbitrarily, and then discover it flags their own good outputs more often than the bad ones.\n\n## The problem this solves\n\nAgent output fails in three distinct ways, and they need different detectors.\n\n**Format failures** are the easy ones. The model returned prose where you needed JSON, omitted a required field, or produced a value outside the allowed enum. These are deterministic and cheap to check. If you are not already validating structured output against a schema, that is the first thing to fix and it is not really the topic of this post.\n\n**Grounding failures** are the interesting middle. The output is well-formed and on-topic, but it asserts something that is not supported by the context it was given. A retrieval-augmented agent cites a document that does not contain the claim. A tool-using agent reports a number that does not match what the tool returned. This is where most silent damage happens, because the output looks exactly like a correct one.\n\n**Policy failures** are the ones that get you in trouble with someone else. The output contains PII it should not have echoed, gives medical or legal advice the product is not licensed to give, or reproduces a competitor's trademark in a way your legal team will ask about. These are partly deterministic (regex for obvious patterns) and partly judgment calls.\n\nA quality gate is a function that takes the agent's output plus its context and returns a verdict — pass, flag, or block — along with the reason. The reason matters as much as the verdict, because you will need it to debug the gate itself.\n\n## Prerequisites and what you'll build\n\nYou need Python 3.10 or later and an API key for whatever model provider you already use. The example uses the OpenAI Python SDK's chat completions interface, which is the most widely mirrored shape across providers — if you are on a different provider, the swap is usually a base URL and a model name.\n\nWhat you will build:\n\n1. A `Check` protocol so each detector is a small, independently testable unit.\n2. Three deterministic checks: schema validation, PII pattern scan, and a groundedness check that compares claims against the retrieved context.\n3. One model-based judge for the cases the deterministic checks cannot decide.\n4. A runner that executes checks in cost order and short-circuits on the first block.\n5. Logging that records every verdict so you can measure the gate's own false-positive rate.\n\nInstall what you need:\n\n```bash\npip install openai pydantic\n```\n\nPydantic is doing the schema work. It is a well-documented library and the version matters — Pydantic v2 changed the validator API significantly from v1, so pin it in your requirements file.\n\n## Step 1 — define the contract before the checks\n\nThe mistake to avoid here is writing detectors against free-form output. If the agent can return anything, every check needs its own parser and they will disagree about what the output even is.\n\nDefine a Pydantic model for the expected output shape. This is your contract.\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\nfrom typing import Literal\n\nclass AgentOutput(BaseModel):\n    answer: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=4000)\n    confidence: Literal[\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]\n    cited_sources: list[str] = Field(default_factory=list)\n```\n\nThe `confidence` field is doing real work. Asking the model to self-report confidence is unreliable as a signal on its own — models are systematically overconfident — but it is useful as a routing hint. A `low` confidence answer with a `high` confidence answer should not go through the same gate.\n\nIf your agent already returns structured output via a provider's JSON mode or function calling, you have this for free. If it returns prose, wrap it: one cheap extraction call to force the shape, then validate. The extraction call is a real cost, but it is far cheaper than discovering downstream that you cannot parse the output at all.\n\n## Step 2 — write deterministic checks first\n\nThe ordering principle: run the cheapest check that can produce a verdict first, and stop as soon as you have one. A PII regex costs effectively nothing; a model-based judge costs a full inference call. If the regex already blocks the output, the judge is wasted money.\n\nDefine a small protocol so checks are uniform:\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\n@dataclass\nclass Verdict:\n    action: str  # \"pass\", \"flag\", \"block\"\n    reason: str\n    check_name: str\n\nclass Check(Protocol):\n    name: str\n    def run(self, output: AgentOutput, context: str) -> Verdict | None:\n        \"\"\"Return None to abstain, a Verdict to decide.\"\"\"\n        ...\n```\n\nThe `None` return is the important design choice. A check that cannot decide should abstain, not default to `pass`. Defaulting to pass is how gates end up rubber-stamping everything.\n\nHere is a PII scanner. This is deliberately crude — it is a first filter, not a compliance system:\n\n```python\nimport re\n\nclass PIICheck:\n    name = \"pii_scan\"\n\n    # Illustrative patterns. Tune to your jurisdiction and data.\n    PATTERNS = {\n        \"email\": re.compile(r\"[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[\\w.]+\"),\n        \"phone_na\": re.compile(r\"\\b\\d{3}[-.]\\d{3}[-.]\\d{4}\\b\"),\n        \"ssn_like\": re.compile(r\"\\b\\d{3}-\\d{2}-\\d{4}\\b\"),\n    }\n\n    def run(self, output: AgentOutput, context: str) -> Verdict | None:\n        hits = [name for name, pat in self.PATTERNS.items()\n                if pat.search(output.answer)]\n        if hits:\n            return Verdict(\"block\", f\"pii patterns: {','.join(hits)}\", self.name)\n        return None\n```\n\nTwo things to be honest about. First, this will produce false positives — a support agent quoting a customer's own email back to them is not a leak, but this check cannot tell the difference. That is why the verdict is `block` with a reason string rather than a silent drop: you need to be able to audit it. Second, regex PII detection has well-documented gaps; names, addresses, and non-US phone formats will slip through. Treat it as a seatbelt, not a cage.\n\n## Step 3 — the groundedness check\n\nThis is the check that catches the failure mode the post is about. The output is well-formed, passes the regexes, and is still wrong because it claims something the context does not support.\n\nThe cheap version: for each sentence in the answer that contains a factual claim, check whether the cited source actually appears in the context. This catches fabricated citations, which are the most common and most embarrassing grounding failure.\n\n```python\nclass CitationCheck:\n    name = \"citation_grounding\"\n\n    def run(self, output: AgentOutput, context: str) -> Verdict | None:\n        if not output.cited_sources:\n            # No citations to verify. Abstain — let the judge decide.\n            return None\n        missing = [s for s in output.cited_sources if s not in context]\n        if missing:\n            return Verdict(\n                \"block\",\n                f\"cited sources not in context: {missing}\",\n                self.name,\n            )\n        return None\n```\n\nThe trap here is substring matching. If the agent cites `\"report.pdf\"` and the context contains `\"report.pdf\"` inside a longer path, this passes. If the agent cites a title and the context contains the title with different whitespace, this fails a legitimate citation. Normalize before comparing — strip, lowercase, collapse whitespace — and accept that you will still get some noise.\n\nThe expensive version compares claims, not citations. Split the answer into sentences, embed each one, and check that the maximum cosine similarity against any context sentence exceeds a threshold. This catches the case where the citation is real but the claim attached to it is not. It costs an embedding call per sentence. For most solo-built products, the citation check plus the judge below is sufficient; add embeddings only if you have measured that citation-checking misses real failures.\n\n## Step 4 — the model-based judge\n\nThe judge runs only on outputs that survived the deterministic checks. Its job is to answer one question: is this answer supported by the context, and does it comply with the stated policy?\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\nJUDGE_PROMPT = \"\"\"You are a quality gate for an AI agent.\nGiven the CONTEXT and the AGENT OUTPUT, decide:\n- supported: does every factual claim follow from the context?\n- policy_ok: does the output avoid giving regulated advice,\n  echoing PII, or making promises the product cannot keep?\n\nRespond with JSON only: {\"supported\": bool, \"policy_ok\": bool,\n\"reason\": \"<one sentence>\"}\n\"\"\"\n\nclass JudgeCheck:\n    name = \"model_judge\"\n\n    def run(self, output: AgentOutput, context: str) -> Verdict | None:\n        resp = client.chat.completions.create(\n            model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n            response_format={\"type\": \"json_object\"},\n            messages=[\n                {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": JUDGE_PROMPT},\n                {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\":\n                 f\"CONTEXT:\\n{context}\\n\\nAGENT OUTPUT:\\n{output.answer}\"},\n            ],\n            temperature=0,\n        )\n        try:\n            verdict = json.loads(resp.choices[0].message.content)\n        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n            return Verdict(\"flag\", \"judge returned unparseable JSON\", self.name)\n\n        if not verdict.get(\"supported\"):\n            return Verdict(\"block\", verdict.get(\"reason\", \"unsupported\"), self.name)\n        if not verdict.get(\"policy_ok\"):\n            return Verdict(\"flag\", verdict.get(\"reason\", \"policy\"), self.name)\n        return None\n```\n\nThree things to get right. Set `temperature=0` — you want the judge to be as deterministic as it can be, or your gate's behavior will drift between runs. Use a smaller, cheaper model for the judge than for the agent; the judge's task is narrower and does not need the strongest model. And always parse defensively: JSON mode reduces but does not eliminate malformed output, and a judge that crashes on bad JSON is worse than no judge.\n\nA common trap: using the same model for the agent and the judge. If the agent hallucinated because the model has a blind spot, the judge with the same weights may share it. Using a different model family for the judge is a cheap way to decorrelate the failures.\n\n## Step 5 — the runner and its ordering\n\n```python\nCHECKS = [PIICheck(), CitationCheck(), JudgeCheck()]\n\ndef gate(output: AgentOutput, context: str) -> Verdict:\n    for check in CHECKS:\n        verdict = check.run(output, context)\n        if verdict and verdict.action == \"block\":\n            return verdict\n    for check in CHECKS:\n        verdict = check.run(output, context)\n        if verdict and verdict.action == \"flag\":\n            return verdict\n    return Verdict(\"pass\", \"all checks passed\", \"runner\")\n```\n\nThe two-pass structure is deliberate but naive — it runs every check twice. In practice you would cache each check's verdict on the first pass. The point is the ordering rule: blocks short-circuit, flags accumulate.\n\n## How to verify it works\n\nA gate you have not tested is a gate you do not have. Build a small labeled set — a few dozen examples is enough to start — with three buckets: known-good outputs, known-bad outputs, and borderline cases. Run the gate over them and record, for each check, how many good outputs it blocked and how many bad outputs it let through.\n\nYou are looking for two numbers per check: false-positive rate on the good bucket and false-negative rate on the bad bucket. A check that blocks good outputs will get disabled by whoever is on call; a check that misses bad outputs is decoration. There is no universal acceptable threshold — it depends on what a wrong answer costs you — but you cannot choose a threshold you have not measured.\n\nLog every verdict with the check name, the reason, and a hash of the output. When a customer reports a bad answer, you want to be able to answer: did the gate see it, and what did it decide?\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**How do I detect hallucination in agent output?**\n\nYou cannot detect it directly, because a hallucination is indistinguishable from a correct answer without external ground truth. What you can do is check grounding: does every claim follow from the context the agent was given? That is the CitationCheck and JudgeCheck above. It will not catch a claim that is true but unsupported, and it will not catch a claim that is wrong in a way the context also gets wrong. It catches the common case, which is a claim the context does not support at all.\n\n**What is a good false-positive rate for an LLM judge?**\n\nThere is no published number that applies to your product, and anyone quoting one is quoting a different task. The useful approach is relative: measure your judge against your deterministic checks on the same labeled set. If the judge's false-positive rate is higher than the check it is replacing, it is not earning its cost. Most teams find that a judge with a tight, specific prompt beats a general one, because the general prompt invites the judge to invent reasons to block.\n\n**Should the quality gate block output or just flag it?**\n\nBlock when a wrong answer causes irreversible harm — sending money, deleting data, giving regulated advice. Flag when a human can review before the output is used. The mistake is defaulting everything to block, which trains operators to bypass the gate, or everything to flag, which means nothing is ever reviewed. Reserve blocks for the small set of failures you would page someone about.\n\n**How much does a model-based judge add to latency?**\n\nIt adds one inference call per gated output. If your agent already makes several calls, the judge roughly adds one more to the chain. The mitigation is the ordering rule: run deterministic checks first so the judge only sees outputs that passed them. If the judge is still too slow, run it asynchronously and flag rather than block — you trade immediate protection for throughput.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\nThe gate above is deliberately minimal. The next additions, in order of value: cache judge verdicts by output hash so identical outputs are not re-judged; add a per-check timeout so a slow check cannot stall the pipeline; and wire the verdict log into a dashboard so you can see the block rate over time. A block rate that jumps is a signal that something upstream changed — a prompt edit, a model version bump, a new retrieval source.\n\nStart with one action: open your agent's output-handling code and find the single place where the output is currently trusted. Add a `PIICheck` and a `CitationCheck` there, log the verdicts to a file, and run your existing test suite through it. You will have a measured false-positive rate for two checks before you write a single line of judge code, and that number will tell you whether the judge is worth building at all.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
